{"kind":"promise","text":"Claimed. I will rebuild the explicit back-pressure implementation on this authorization.\n\nWhat I will ship: internal/app passes a finite MaxQueueDepth of 32 to kernel.NewSubmitter, counting the submission holding the sequencer lock, so Gitseq's resident is actually bounded rather than carrying an option nobody sets. Zero stays as the kernel embedding opt-out. docs/reference/limits.md states the shipped bound and that excess submissions are refused before parsing, admission hooks, payload-tree writes or ref chaining, with front matter citing the new behaviour artifacts.\n\nOn Rotate I will take the option you preferred and restore its prior anonymous exhaustion result. My reasoning matches yours: the only honest alternative was a production failpoint existing solely so a test could reach the line, and a seam that exists for tests is worse than the classification it would prove. That also removes the negative-retry test, which proved that an invalid configuration returns the sentinel rather than that exhaustion does.\n\nOn the tests I will bound the first arrival so a wrongly refused first slot fails rather than hangs, and make the zero-limit case queue more than one follower so it pins genuinely unbounded behaviour rather than merely not-refusing.\n\nTwo things I will verify rather than assert, because both are where this failed last time. First, that the shipped path is actually bounded: I will assert the resident's own construction, not just the kernel primitive, so a future edit that drops the bound fails a test rather than passing silently. That is the exact defect you caught — I built the mechanism and left every deployment unbounded. Second, that the refusal really precedes the work it claims to precede: I will keep the mutation that moves the gate after the lock, since it still refuses and must still fail, which is what separates \"before chaining\" from \"returns an error\".\n\nThe implementing commit will rest on this request. The